---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

export const prerender = true;

const title = "About Us | Fuel Calculate";
const description =
  "About Fuel Calculate: a free fuel calculator for trip cost, fuel comparison, and monthly budgets across India, the US, UK, Canada and Australia — and how honest we are about where the numbers come from.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: "Home", href: "/" }, { name: "About", href: "/about" }]} />
  <Hero eyebrow="About us" title="A fuel calculator built to give you a real number." />

  <section class="mx-auto flex max-w-[720px] flex-col gap-2xl px-md pb-5xl sm:px-lg">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">What Fuel Calculate is</h2>
      <p class="mt-sm text-body-md text-body">
        Fuel Calculate is a free fuel calculator for working out what a trip actually
        costs — for a car or a bike, on petrol, diesel, CNG, E20 or EV. Pick a city in
        India, or press "Use my location" in the other four markets, so a sourced local
        price fills in; choose your vehicle from a database of hundreds of models or
        enter your own mileage, and get a real per-distance cost instead of a
        rounded-off guess. Beyond a single trip,
        the comparison tool weighs two fuels or vehicles side by side, and the
        monthly projection tool turns a daily commute into a real monthly and yearly
        budget.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-sm text-body-md text-body">
        The site now covers five markets — <a href="/" class="text-link underline">India</a>,
        <a href="/us/" class="text-link underline">the United States</a>,
        <a href="/gb/" class="text-link underline">the United Kingdom</a>,
        <a href="/ca/" class="text-link underline">Canada</a>, and
        <a href="/au/" class="text-link underline">Australia</a> — each with its own
        currency, units, and fuel types, so the numbers make sense wherever you are.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Why it exists</h2>
      <p class="mt-sm text-body-md text-body">
        Most fuel cost calculators stop at a single national-average figure. That's
        rarely the number you actually need — your car's real mileage, your city's
        real pump price, and your actual trip distance all matter more than an
        average. Fuel Calculate was built to use your specific numbers wherever
        possible, and to be upfront whenever it can't: every mileage figure is
        editable, because the tool's own presets are a starting point, not the final
        word.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Where the numbers come from</h2>
      <p class="mt-sm text-body-md text-body">
        We'd rather tell you exactly how reliable a figure is than let you assume.
        <strong>Fuel prices.</strong> India's city prices are refreshed automatically
        from public sources several times a day. In the other markets every price shown
        is a dated figure from a named public source: AAA state averages in the US,
        the UK government's weekly road fuel prices, Natural Resources Canada's daily
        averages, and the Australian Institute of Petroleum's weekly city averages.
        Each is a snapshot, not a live feed, and the page shows its date; the
        <a href="/us/" class="text-link underline">country pages</a> link every source.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-sm text-body-md text-body">
        <strong>Vehicle mileage.</strong> India's figures are ARAI-claimed
        (official test-cycle) data, and the US list uses EPA-style ratings. The UK,
        Canada and Australia vehicle lists are our own representative estimates of
        common models, not manufacturer data, and are labelled that way. Electricity
        rates are typical residential estimates unless a page cites a regional figure.
        Nothing on this site is a substitute for checking your own vehicle's documented
        mileage or the price at the pump before you rely on a number for anything that
        matters financially — every figure is editable for exactly that reason. See the
        <a href="/how-it-works" class="text-link underline">math behind the results</a>
        and the <a href="/faq" class="text-link underline">FAQ</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-display-sm text-ink">Who runs it</h2>
      <p class="mt-sm text-body-md text-body">
        Fuel Calculate is built and maintained independently, not by a large company
        or a funded startup — which is also why we're direct about which data is
        solid and which is still an estimate, rather than presenting everything with
        false confidence. Questions, corrections, or found a mileage figure that's
        off? <a href="/contact" class="text-link underline">Get in touch</a> — see our
        <a href="/privacy-policy" class="text-link underline">Privacy Policy</a> and
        <a href="/terms" class="text-link underline">Terms &amp; Conditions</a> for
        how the site handles your data and what using it means.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
